## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates your ability to integrate platform-specific native compute (Metal/Vulkan) with React Native, manage tight-latency streaming paths, and implement proper memory management in a real-time UI context.

## Key Concepts
- **TurboModule/JSI**: High-performance JavaScript-Native bridging for minimal overhead
- **Dedicated native thread**: Compute-intensive work runs off the main thread to maintain UI responsiveness
- **Platform-specific shaders**: Metal (iOS) vs Vulkan (Android) for GPU-accelerated denoising
- **Bounded queue with backpressure**: Prevents memory exhaustion by controlling frame accumulation
- **Memory pool allocation**: Buffer alignment and zero-copy transfers for optimal performance

## Usage Example
```javascript
const denoiseId = await RealTimeDenoiser.startDenoise({
  width: 1920,
  height: 1080,
  quality: 'high'
});

RealTimeDenoiser.onFrame(denoiseId, (processedBuffer) => {
  // Handle denoised frame
  updatePreview(processedBuffer);
});

// In camera frame callback
camera.onFrame = (frame) => {
  RealTimeDenoiser.pushFrame(denoiseId, frame.buffer, frame.timestamp);
};

// Cleanup
await RealTimeDenoiser.stopDenoise(denoiseId);
```

## Memory Management Strategy
- Pre-allocate frame buffers using memory pools to avoid repeated allocations
- Use pinned/zero-copy buffers where possible to eliminate data copying between native and GPU memory
- Implement reference counting to safely release buffers when frames are consumed
- Monitor memory pressure and dynamically adjust queue capacity if needed

## Frame Dropping Strategy
- When the bounded queue reaches capacity, drop the oldest unprocessed frame (FIFO eviction)
- This approach prevents camera pipeline blocking while ensuring the most recent frames are processed
- Consider adding a frame timestamp check to detect and handle significant lag (e.g., drop frames older than 500ms)

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests cross-platform native module design, real-time constraints, memory management, and safe bridging with JavaScript.

## Key Concepts
- Real-time audio pipeline, native threads, DSP paths, and cross-platform bridging
- Lock-free data structures, sample-rate negotiation, backpressure handling, and deterministic scheduling

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal API usage sketch
import { startEngine, feedInput, onProcessed, stopEngine } from 'NativeAudio';

startEngine({ sampleRate: 48000, channels: 1 });
feedInput(inputBuffer, length);
onProcessed((processedBuffer) => { /* handle processed audio */ });
stopEngine();
```

## Architecture Considerations
- **Threading**: Run audio processing on a dedicated high-priority thread (real-time priority on Android, a dedicated dispatch queue on iOS) separate from the JS thread to avoid blocking.
- **Memory**: Use lock-free ring buffers and pre-allocated buffer pools to ensure zero allocations on the hot path.
- **Backpressure**: Implement a bounded queue with clear semantics for what happens when the input buffer fills—either drop frames, signal overflow, or block gracefully based on the use case.
- **Sample-rate changes**: Negotiate sample rate at engine start; if the device doesn't support the requested rate, fall back to the nearest supported rate and notify JS.
- **Fallback**: Detect DSP capability at runtime; if unavailable, either gracefully degrade or expose a clear error indicating the device lacks required hardware support.

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests practical RN skills: list rendering, FlatList optimization, local filtering, and simple interactivity with pull-to-refresh. It checks understanding of render perf basics for beginner level in a real app.

## Key Concepts

- FlatList basics and keyExtractor
- React.memo for item rows
- useMemo for derived data
- Debounced search input
- Pull-to-refresh with refreshControl

## Code Example

```javascript
import React, {useState, useMemo, useCallback} from 'react';
import {FlatList, TextInput, View, Text, RefreshControl} from 'react-native';

const ContactRow = React.memo(({c}) => (
  <View style={{padding:8}}>
    <Text>{c.firstName} {c.lastName}</Text>
    <Text>{c.phone}</Text>
  </View>
));

export default function Contacts({data}) {
  const [q, setQ] = useState('');
  const [refreshing, setRefreshing] = useState(false);

  const filtered = useMemo(() => data.filter(c => {
    const n = (c.firstName + ' ' + c.lastName).toLowerCase();
    return n.includes(q.toLowerCase());
  }), [data, q]);

  const onRefresh = useCallback(() => {
    setRefreshing(true);
    setTimeout(() => setRefreshing(false), 1000);
  }, []);

  return (
    <FlatList
      data={filtered}
      keyExtractor={item => item.id}
      renderItem={({item}) => <ContactRow c={item} />}
      refreshControl={<RefreshControl refreshing={refreshing} onRefresh={onRefresh} />}
    />
  );
}
```

